(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-pdm-exam-2-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 55-year-old's lipid values are entered into a cardiovascular risk equation. What does that step produce?","choices":["A diagnosis of acute infarction","A 10-year risk estimate","A measured LDL-C value","A lifetime lipoprotein(a) level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lipid values feed 10-year risk estimation, with the PREVENT (Predicting Risk of cardiovascular disease EVENTs) equations in the current guideline, and the estimate then drives treatment intensity. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 55-year-old presents two days after an episode of chest pain. Why is troponin preferred over CK-MB (creatine kinase muscle-brain isoenzyme)?","choices":["It rises later and clears more quickly","It is also raised by skeletal muscle injury","It is cheaper and needs no clinical context","It rises sooner and stays elevated longer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That expands the window for diagnosis and treatment, which matters for a patient presenting late. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is cephalization on a chest radiograph?","choices":["Fluid tracking into the interlobar fissures","The trachea deviating toward the apex","Upper zone vessels becoming larger","Calcification of the aortic arch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flow redistributes upward, which is why the sign sits in the lungs while the problem is in the heart. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 35-year-old has lipoprotein(a) measured for the first time. How often does it generally need measuring?","choices":["Every year","Every 5 years","Once in a lifetime","After every statin change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is more than 90% genetically determined and stable over life, and needs no fasting, so a single measurement generally suffices. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What distinguishes the relative from the absolute refractory period?","choices":["In the relative period no stimulus of any strength will produce a response","In the relative period a sufficiently strong stimulus can produce a response","The relative period occurs before the absolute period","The relative period occupies the whole of phase 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the dangerous window: the cell is excitable again but not yet fully recovered, so a stimulus arriving there can start something abnormal. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 44-year-old asks what the normal LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) is. What is the accurate answer?","choices":["Below 200 mg/dL for everyone","Below 150 mg/dL for everyone","The goal depends on her risk tier","Any value while HDL-C is high"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adult LDL-C and non-HDL-C have no single normal value; the goal depends on the patient's atherosclerotic risk tier, whereas triglyceride, apoB (apolipoprotein B) and lipoprotein(a) thresholds are more fixed. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A properly performed posteroanterior chest radiograph shows the cardiac silhouette occupying 58 percent of the internal thoracic diameter. What does that suggest?","choices":["A normal heart size","Pulmonary hypertension without cardiac enlargement","Cardiomegaly","A pericardial effusion specifically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The silhouette should normally occupy no more than about half the internal thoracic diameter, so this exceeds it. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does cardiovascular magnetic resonance map to build its three-dimensional images?","choices":["Iodinated contrast pooling in vessels","Reflected high-frequency sound pulses","Gamma rays emitted by an injected tracer","Hydrogen atoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Strong magnetic fields and radiofrequency pulses are used to map hydrogen atoms, giving very detailed anatomy of the heart and great vessels. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How is the heart stressed for a nuclear perfusion study in a patient who cannot exercise?","choices":["Pharmacologically, with an agent such as adenosine","By transesophageal pacing","By cold pressor testing","The study cannot be performed without exercise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The stress can be produced by exercise or by drug, and the imaging is the same either way. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Why does the cardiothoracic ratio require a properly performed posteroanterior film?","choices":["Only a posteroanterior film shows the pulmonary vasculature","Projection alters the apparent size of the cardiac silhouette","Contrast is required and is only given for posteroanterior films","The ratio can only be calculated with electrocardiographic gating"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A ratio measured off the wrong projection is a measurement of the technique rather than of the heart. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which relationship is checked when assessing P waves on a strip?","choices":["Whether there is a P wave for every QRS complex and a QRS for every P wave","Whether the P wave is taller than the T wave","Whether the P wave falls within the QT interval","Whether the P wave is visible in all twelve leads"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a two-way check, and each direction fails in a different way, which is what makes the blocks distinguishable. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the ionic arrangement during phase 4, the resting state?","choices":["More sodium inside the cell, with potassium outside","Equal concentrations of all ions across the membrane","Calcium inside the cell with potassium outside","More potassium inside the cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That separation is the stored charge the cell spends when it fires, and restoring it is what the later phases achieve. With sodium and calcium outside. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which is an indication for cardiac computed tomography rather than the angiographic study?","choices":["Detecting and excluding coronary stenosis","Assessing exercise capacity","Evaluating deep venous thrombosis","Pre-operative planning before cardiac surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside calcium deposits, coronary anatomical anomalies and congenital disease, and it is usually done immediately before the angiographic study. Particularly valvular and great vessel procedures. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the echocardiogram looking for after exercise, and why must it be obtained quickly?","choices":["Valve gradients, which take time to develop","Pericardial fluid, which accumulates after exertion","Coronary calcium, which is best seen at high heart rates","Abnormal left ventricular wall motion induced by exercise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The finding is transient, so the patient is asked to dismount quickly and the heart is imaged before it settles. Which resolves as the heart recovers. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman takes a direct oral anticoagulant and has a normal prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time. What does this mean for her bleeding risk?","choices":["Bleeding risk is excluded","Routine level monitoring is required","The drug has no anticoagulant effect","Bleeding risk is still increased"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most direct oral anticoagulants may not derange the prothrombin time or partial thromboplastin time yet still increase bleeding risk, and routine monitoring is not required. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with recurrent thrombosis is evaluated for a hypercoagulable state. Which deficiency is a primary cause tested?","choices":["Hemophilia A","Protein C deficiency","Immune thrombocytopenia","Factor VII deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary hypercoagulable causes include deficiencies of antithrombin III, protein C and protein S, along with abnormal fibrinolytic mechanisms. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 48-year-old asks why cholesterol travels in the blood inside particles. What is the reason?","choices":["Cholesterol and triglycerides are insoluble","Cholesterol is broken down by plasma enzymes","Particles let the kidney filter cholesterol","Free cholesterol would raise the troponin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The major lipids, cholesterol and triglycerides, are insoluble in plasma, so they travel packaged inside lipoproteins. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has easy bruising and bleeding gums. Which initial set of studies is appropriate?","choices":["Prothrombin time, activated partial thromboplastin time and D-dimer","D-dimer and fibrin monomers only","Blood count with smear, clotting times and fibrinogen","Bleeding time alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The bleeding workup is a blood count with differential and peripheral smear, prothrombin time with international normalized ratio, activated partial thromboplastin time and fibrinogen. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What must a deflection do to count as a true S wave?","choices":["Follow the T wave","Exceed one small box in amplitude","Extend below the baseline","Appear in every lead"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If it does not, it is called an S wave pattern instead, which is a distinction of naming that matters when describing a tracing precisely. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Lead I and lead aVL form a contiguous group with which other leads?","choices":["V5 and V6","V1 and V2","II and III","V3 and V4"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leads I, aVL, V5 and V6 look at the same area of the heart, so a change across all four means more than one isolated lead. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which structure is described as the third pacemaker, taking over if both the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes fail?","choices":["The bundle of His","The left anterior fascicle","The Purkinje network","The internodal tracts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, at an intrinsic rate of twenty to forty -- slow enough to be a last resort rather than a comfortable substitute. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which aortic conditions is cardiovascular magnetic resonance used to diagnose and monitor?","choices":["The coronary artery calcium burden","Aneurysm, dissection, hematoma, coarctation","Valve vegetations","Deep venous thrombosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All four are questions about the wall and the lumen of a large vessel, which is where its detailed anatomy is most useful. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has heavy menses and easy bruising. Which inherited bleeding disorder is the most common overall?","choices":["Hemophilia A","Hemophilia B","Factor XIII deficiency","von Willebrand disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. von Willebrand disease is the most common inherited bleeding disorder, and it is tested with von Willebrand factor antigen, activity and a factor VIII level. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"On standard calibration, a deflection rises ten millimeters, which is two large boxes. What voltage does it represent?","choices":["1 millivolt","10 millivolts","0.1 millivolt","0.5 millivolt"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Standard calibration sets two large boxes, a 10 millimeter deflection, equal to 1 millivolt, so each small box is 0.1 millivolt. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What happens during phase 0, the upstroke?","choices":["Calcium channels open and calcium enters the cell","Fast sodium channels open and sodium rushes into the cell","Potassium channels open and potassium leaves the cell","Sodium is actively pumped out of the cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the fastest event in the cycle, and the rapid inward sodium current is what drives the voltage positive. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which limitations are described for vascular ultrasound?","choices":["Ionizing radiation dose and contrast nephrotoxicity","Highly operator dependent, limited by body habitus","Dependence on heart rate and rhythm","Inability to show blood flow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calcification causes acoustic shadowing, which matters because the vessels most worth imaging are often the ones most calcified. Degraded by bone, air and calcification. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has a D-dimer reported. Which three enzymes act to form this cross-linked fibrin degradation product?","choices":["Thrombin, tissue factor and factor VII","Plasmin, antithrombin and protein C","Factor Xa, factor IXa and factor VIIIa","Thrombin, factor XIII and plasmin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer results from the action of thrombin (forming fibrin), activated factor XIII (cross-linking it) and plasmin (degrading it). — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man gets a standard lipid panel. Which components are measured directly?","choices":["LDL-C, non-HDL-C and triglycerides","Total cholesterol, HDL-C and triglycerides","Total cholesterol, LDL-C and non-HDL-C","LDL-C, HDL-C and total cholesterol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those three are measured. LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) and non-HDL-C are calculated, so the LDL-C on a standard report is an estimate. HDL-C is high-density lipoprotein cholesterol. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 39-year-old is found to have a variant of an apolipoprotein that is critical in triglyceride clearance. Which apolipoprotein is that?","choices":["ApoA-II (apolipoprotein A-II)","ApoB-48 (apolipoprotein B-48)","ApoE (apolipoprotein E)","Apo(a) (apolipoprotein(a))"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ApoE is important in triglyceride metabolism and critical in triglyceride clearance. ApoC, apoC-III and apoA-V regulate triglyceride metabolism. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man bleeds, and a physician reviews platelet counts. Which statement about count and bleeding is correct?","choices":["Only low counts cause bleeding","Only high counts cause bleeding","Counts never cause bleeding with a normal prothrombin time","Very low and very high counts can both cause bleeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thrombocytopenia and extreme thrombocythemia, above 1,000 times 10 to the ninth per liter, can both cause bleeding. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has an isolated prolonged partial thromboplastin time. Before further evaluation, which effect must be ruled out first?","choices":["Heparin","Warfarin","Aspirin","Platelet clumping"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The algorithm for an isolated prolonged partial thromboplastin time begins by ruling out heparin effect. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a low automated platelet count. His smear shows platelet clumps and platelets surrounding neutrophils. What does this indicate?","choices":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","A falsely low count","Immune thrombocytopenia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clumping and platelets ringing neutrophils (satellitism) make the automated count read falsely low, which is spurious thrombocytopenia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has an acute coronary syndrome confirmed. Besides troponin, which biomarker also carries prognostic value after the event?","choices":["Creatine kinase","Myoglobin","Lactate dehydrogenase","B-type natriuretic peptide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. B-type natriuretic peptide or its N-terminal fragment supports heart failure diagnosis and triage and is also prognostic after acute coronary syndrome. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does the QRS complex represent, and what should it look like?","choices":["Ventricular repolarization, broad and rounded","Ventricular depolarization, narrow with sharp points","Atrial depolarization, narrow and upright","The plateau phase of the action potential"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with a duration under three small boxes. A wide QRS means the ventricles were not activated through the normal fast pathway. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the hexaxial reference system used to determine?","choices":["The normal axis of the heart","The heart rate","The horizontal view of the heart","The duration of the QT interval"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Six views in one plane are what make an axis calculable at all. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 50-year-old in primary prevention has borderline cardiovascular risk. When is high-sensitivity C-reactive protein appropriate?","choices":["To refine atherosclerotic risk","To diagnose acute infarction","To triage acute dyspnea","To detect statin myopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is an inflammatory risk-enhancing marker in select primary-prevention patients, not a diagnostic test for an acute cardiac event. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman is taught the three overlapping stages of hemostasis. Which set is correct?","choices":["Primary hemostasis, secondary hemostasis, fibrinolysis","Primary hemostasis, secondary hemostasis, thrombosis","Platelet plug, fibrin clot, embolism","Coagulation, thrombosis, embolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemostasis has three overlapping stages: the platelet plug (primary hemostasis), the fibrin clot built by the coagulation factors (secondary hemostasis) and the breakdown of the clot (fibrinolysis). — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has hemarthroses and hematoma after surgery, with a normal prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time and platelet count. Which next test fits?","choices":["Factor VIII assay","Euglobulin clot lysis time","A mixing study","Platelet aggregation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This picture suggests deficiency of inhibitors of the fibrinolytic system, evaluated with euglobulin clot lysis time and testing for alpha-2 antiplasmin and plasminogen activator inhibitor 1. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does the peak of the T wave mark?","choices":["The end of ventricular depolarization","The start of atrial repolarization","The beginning of the ST segment","The dividing line between the absolute and the relative refractory period"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is what makes a stimulus landing near the T wave peak so consequential: the cell has just become excitable again. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In which position does a patient lie initially for transthoracic echocardiography?","choices":["Supine and flat throughout","Left lateral decubitus","Prone","Standing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It brings the heart toward the chest wall, and the patient is moved through other positions to obtain the different views. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the intrinsic firing rate of the sinoatrial node, the main pacemaker of the heart?","choices":["40 to 60 beats per minute","20 to 40 beats per minute","60 to 100 beats per minute","100 to 150 beats per minute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the fastest intrinsic rate in the conduction system, which is why the sinoatrial node normally sets the pace and the slower pacemakers stay in reserve. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which physical limb electrode was added to the original three to give four limb electrodes?","choices":["Right arm","Right leg","Left arm","Left leg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The right leg electrode was added to the original three (right arm, left arm, left leg). It serves as a ground, and the four physical limb electrodes give the six hexaxial leads. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has worsening dyspnea and leg edema. What does a raised B-type natriuretic peptide in her blood reflect?","choices":["Myocyte necrosis","Systemic inflammation","Skeletal muscle breakdown","Myocardial wall stretch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. B-type natriuretic peptide is released by the ventricles with volume or pressure overload. It reflects stretch rather than injury, a different mechanism from troponin, so the two are complementary. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has an isolated prolonged partial thromboplastin time that corrects on mixing, with a low factor VIII and normal von Willebrand antigen and activity. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["von Willebrand disease","Lupus anticoagulant","Hemophilia","Dysfibrinogenemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A low factor VIII with normal von Willebrand antigen and activity is hemophilia, and with abnormal von Willebrand studies it would be von Willebrand disease. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has a normal platelet count and a bleeding time of 18 minutes (reference 3 to 10). What does this suggest?","choices":["Platelets are not working well","Coagulation factors are deficient","Fibrinolysis is excessive","The platelet count is falsely high"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a normal platelet count a prolonged bleeding time signals a platelet function problem, a primary hemostasis defect. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why is phase 2 called the plateau?","choices":["All ion movement stops entirely","Sodium continues to enter at a constant rate","Calcium entering the cell is matched by potassium moving the other way","The cell has returned to its resting voltage and remains there"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the calcium entering during this balance is what actually produces contraction, so the flat part of the trace is the working part of the beat. So the voltage holds. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 54-year-old's 10-year PREVENT (Predicting Risk of cardiovascular disease EVENTs) estimate is 4%, in the borderline tier of 3% to under 5%. Besides discussing the estimate, what does the framework add for her?","choices":["Evaluating risk enhancers","Immediate cardiac catheterization","Serial troponin measurement","Stopping all lipid testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At borderline risk the framework pairs the risk discussion with an evaluation of risk enhancers, such as an elevated lipoprotein(a) or triglycerides of 150 mg/dL or above. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Why is an intravenous sedative given before a transesophageal echocardiogram?","choices":["To slow the heart for clearer images","To numb the back of the throat","To relax the patient and prevent vomiting","To prevent a reaction to contrast"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sedative helps the patient relax and prevents vomiting while the probe is passed through the mouth and down the esophagus. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How are the catheters for electrophysiological testing introduced?","choices":["Through arteries into the left ventricle","Down the esophagus on an endoscope","Into each coronary artery through the aorta","Through veins into the right heart"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Three or four catheters pass from the internal jugular, subclavian or common femoral vein into the right atrium or right ventricle. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 40-year-old with no risk factors is due a routine lipid profile and has eaten breakfast. Is the sample acceptable?","choices":["Yes, nonfasting is acceptable for most","No, every lipid profile must be fasting","No, lipids can only be drawn at night","Yes, but only lipoprotein(a) is valid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonfasting is acceptable for most people. Fasting is reserved for triglycerides of 400 mg/dL or above, triglyceride disorders, or a family history of premature disease or genetic dyslipidemia. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a fibrinogen of 0.4 grams per liter (reference 2.0 to 4.0) before traumatic surgery. What is the concern?","choices":["Coronary artery disease","Hemorrhage after surgery","Deep vein thrombosis","Platelet clumping"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A fibrinogen below 0.5 grams per liter can result in hemorrhage after traumatic surgery; low fibrinogen means bleeding. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 23-year-old is brought in after collapsing during a marathon, with dark urine and painful thighs. Which marker best evaluates the suspected problem?","choices":["Troponin I","N-terminal pro B-type natriuretic peptide","Lactate dehydrogenase","Creatine kinase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rhabdomyolysis is skeletal muscle injury, which is exactly where creatine kinase is still used. Its low cardiac specificity is no drawback here. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 58-year-old with diabetes has an LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) already below 70 mg/dL on therapy. Which test helps find remaining risk?","choices":["HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol)","Creatine kinase","ApoB (apolipoprotein B)","Troponin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ApoB is used when triglycerides are 150 mg/dL or above, in diabetes, or when LDL-C is below 70 mg/dL, to find residual risk carried by atherogenic particles. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why is the TP segment the best place to assess the isoelectric line?","choices":["It is the longest segment on the tracing","It contains the J point","It is unaffected by lead placement","It represents the resting state of the cardiac cells, between one T wave and the next P wave"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nothing electrical is happening there, so it gives the truest baseline against which to judge elevation or depression elsewhere. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"On a vascular ultrasound, how does tissue density relate to the image?","choices":["The denser the structure the darker it appears","Density does not affect the image, only depth does","The denser the structure the lighter it appears","Only flowing blood produces any signal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and superficial structures sit nearer the top of the screen, which together let you orient any image without being told what it is. So air is black and bone is near white. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which exercise modalities are used, and which is preferred among the cycles?","choices":["Treadmill and cycle ergometer","Treadmill only","Arm ergometry, as first choice","Step testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and arm ergometry exists for patients who cannot use their legs, which keeps the test available to more people. With the stationary bicycle preferred. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 62-year-old has a troponin above the 99th percentile limit. What else must be present to call it a myocardial infarction?","choices":["A raised creatine kinase as confirmation","Ischemia and a rising or falling pattern","A raised B-type natriuretic peptide","A value at least 10 times the limit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Any troponin elevation is myocardial injury; infarction is injury plus ischemia plus a rising or falling pattern. Keeping the two apart is essential. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has large palpable ecchymoses and bleeding into joints and muscles with hematoma formation. Which category fits?","choices":["Platelet disorder","Clotting factor deficiency","Mild von Willebrand disease","Spurious thrombocytopenia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Large palpable ecchymoses and bleeding into deep soft tissues, joints and muscles with hematoma are typical of a clotting factor deficiency. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A patient stops an exercise stress test having reached 70 percent of their predicted maximum heart rate, with no electrocardiographic changes. How should the result be read?","choices":["Inadequate to exclude ischemia","A negative test, excluding ischemia","A positive test","Equivocal, requiring repeat in six months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The threshold for a valid test is 85 percent, so a negative result below it says nothing about the heart, only about the test. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a prothrombin time of 19 seconds (reference 11 to 13) and a normal activated partial thromboplastin time. Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor XI"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An isolated prolonged prothrombin time points to the extrinsic pathway, factor VII deficiency. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 32"}]};
